<h2>Formulas for general <i>n</i>-dimensional functions</h2>

<table class="formula-table">
  <colgroup>
    <col style="width:40px">
    <col style="width:170px">
    <col style="width:210px">
    <col style="width:210px">
    <col style="width:210px">
    <col style="width:280px">
  </colgroup>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th></th>
      <th>Function</th>
      <th>Fourier transform<br>unitary, ordinary<br>frequency</th>
      <th>Fourier transform<br>unitary, angular<br>frequency</th>
      <th>Fourier transform<br>non-unitary, angular<br>frequency</th>
      <th>Remarks</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td class="num">500</td>
      <td class="func">$f(\mathbf{x})$</td>
      <td>$\widehat{f}_1(\boldsymbol{\xi}) \triangleq \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} f(\mathbf{x}) e^{-i2\pi \boldsymbol{\xi}\cdot\mathbf{x}}\, d\mathbf{x}$</td>
      <td>$\widehat{f}_2(\boldsymbol{\omega}) \triangleq \dfrac{1}{(2\pi)^{\frac{n}{2}}}\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} f(\mathbf{x}) e^{-i\boldsymbol{\omega}\cdot\mathbf{x}}\, d\mathbf{x}$</td>
      <td>$\widehat{f}_3(\boldsymbol{\omega}) \triangleq \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} f(\mathbf{x}) e^{-i\boldsymbol{\omega}\cdot\mathbf{x}}\, d\mathbf{x}$</td>
      <td class="remarks"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="num">501</td>
      <td class="func">$\chi_{[0,1]}(|\mathbf{x}|)\left(1-|\mathbf{x}|^2\right)^{\delta}$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{\Gamma(\delta+1)}{\pi^{\delta}\,|\boldsymbol{\xi}|^{\gamma}} J_{\gamma}(2\pi|\boldsymbol{\xi}|)$</td>
      <td>$2^{\delta}\,\dfrac{\Gamma(\delta+1)}{|\boldsymbol{\omega}|^{\gamma}} J_{\gamma}(|\boldsymbol{\omega}|)$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{\Gamma(\delta+1)}{\pi^{\delta}}\left|\dfrac{\boldsymbol{\omega}}{2\pi}\right|^{-\gamma} J_{\gamma}(|\boldsymbol{\omega}|)$</td>
      <td class="remarks">
        Here, $\gamma = \frac{n}{2} + \delta$.<br><br>
        The function $\chi_{[0,\ 1]}$ is the indicator function of the interval $[0,\ 1]$. The function $\Gamma(x)$ is the gamma function. The function $J_{\frac{n}{2}+\delta}$ is a Bessel function of the first kind, with order $\frac{n}{2} + \delta$. Taking $n = 2$ and $\delta = 0$ produces 402.<sup>[82]</sup>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="num">502</td>
      <td class="func">$|\mathbf{x}|^{-\alpha}, \quad 0 < \operatorname{Re}\alpha < n$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{(2\pi)^{\alpha}}{c_{n,\alpha}}|\boldsymbol{\xi}|^{-(n-\alpha)}$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{(2\pi)^{\frac{n}{2}}}{c_{n,\alpha}}|\boldsymbol{\omega}|^{-(n-\alpha)}$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{(2\pi)^{n}}{c_{n,\alpha}}|\boldsymbol{\omega}|^{-(n-\alpha)}$</td>
      <td class="remarks">
        See <a href="#"><i>Riesz potential</i></a>, where the constant is given by<br>
        $c_{n,\alpha} = \pi^{\frac{n}{2}}2^{\alpha}\Gamma\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}\right)/\Gamma\left(\frac{n-\alpha}{2}\right)$.<br>
        The formula also holds for all $\alpha \neq n, n+2, \dots$ by analytic continuation, but then the function and its Fourier transforms need to be understood as suitably regularized tempered distributions (see <a href="#"><i>Homogeneous distribution</i></a>).<sup>[note 10]</sup>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="num">503</td>
      <td class="func">$\dfrac{1}{|\boldsymbol{\sigma}|(2\pi)^{\frac{n}{2}}} e^{-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{x}^{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-1}\mathbf{x}}$</td>
      <td>$e^{-2\pi^2 \boldsymbol{\xi}^{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\sigma}\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\xi}}$</td>
      <td>$(2\pi)^{-\frac{n}{2}} e^{-\frac{1}{2}\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\sigma}\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\omega}}$</td>
      <td>$e^{-\frac{1}{2}\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\sigma}\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\omega}}$</td>
      <td class="remarks">
        This is the formula for a <a href="#">multivariate normal distribution</a> normalized to 1 with a mean of 0. Bold variables are vectors or matrices. Following the notation of the aforementioned page, $\boldsymbol{\Sigma} = \boldsymbol{\sigma}\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\mathrm{T}}$ and $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} = \boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-1}$
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="num">504</td>
      <td class="func">$e^{-2\pi\alpha|\mathbf{x}|}$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{c_n \alpha}{\left(\alpha^2+|\boldsymbol{\xi}|^2\right)^{\frac{n+1}{2}}}$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{c_n(2\pi)^{\frac{n+2}{2}}\alpha}{\left(4\pi^2\alpha^2+|\boldsymbol{\omega}|^2\right)^{\frac{n+1}{2}}}$</td>
      <td>$\dfrac{c_n(2\pi)^{n+1}\alpha}{\left(4\pi^2\alpha^2+|\boldsymbol{\omega}|^2\right)^{\frac{n+1}{2}}}$</td>
      <td class="remarks">
        Here<sup>[83]</sup> $c_n = \Gamma\left(\dfrac{n+1}{2}\right)/\pi^{\frac{n+1}{2}}$, $\operatorname{Re}(\alpha) > 0$
      </td>
    </tr>
